  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nDry weather through tonight...\n\nLatest IR satellite imagery indicates despite temperatures near\nthe dew point, clear skies, and light winds, fog is not forming \nacross the region. In addition, recent model and ensemble data is \nless bullish on fog formation early this morning other than some \npatchy fog and minor visibility restrictions. Therefore, fog is\nnot expected to have much of an impact this morning or during the\nmorning commute. High pressure will keep the weather dry across\nthe region through tonight.\n\nA cold front brings rain chances Tuesday through Wednesday\nnight...\n\nA cold front sinking south will bring a chance (20-40%) of rain as\nit tracks through the region Tuesday. The initial surge will not\nhave much in the form of dynamics; therefore, precipitation is not\nexpected to be ubiquitous with this first shot. \n\nThe front stalls across the western Great Lakes region Tuesday \nnight as the main surface low lifts north from the southern Plains\nand strong mid level frontogenesis develops along the front. This\nwill bring an increased chance (50-80%) for showers and \nthunderstorms Tuesday night across east-central Wisconsin and the \nlakeshore. Modest instability (MUCAPEs of 500-1000 J/kg) and shear\n(25-30 knots) should keep storms below severe limits. PWATs up to\n2 inches and long skinny CAPE profiles will mean excessive rain \nwill be a threat, especially Tuesday night across eastern \nWisconsin.\n\nThe front will sink a bit farther southeast as a mid level trough\nand attendant shortwave track through the western Great Lakes.\nThis will bring a continued chance (30-50%) for additional showers\nand thunderstorms across this same area Wednesday and Wednesday\nnight. Given the front will be a bit farther southeast, the\nadditional rainfall should mainly be a glancing blow during this\nperiod with the heaviest rain expected to be southeast. Continued\nhigh PWATs of 1.5 to 1.75 will mean excessive rainfall will still\nbe a threat, especially for areas that receive repeated rounds of\nheavy rain during this period.\n\nDry weather expected late in the week with active weather again\nfor the weekend...\n\nHigh pressure is then expected to bring dry weather Thursday and\nFriday across the area. Another cold front sinking south will\nbring additional chances (20-40%) for the upcoming weekend. NWP\nmodels and ensembles are murkier for the upcoming weekend as\ntiming and placement of the upcoming thunderstorms across the\nregion differ quite a bit. Therefore, the entire weekend will\nneither be completely dry or a complete washout.",
